  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nSurface high pressure over the area with only some thin high level \nclouds spilling into the area early this morning. Cooler temperatures\nwill be observed this morning with readings ranging from the upper \n30s east of Columbus to the lower 40s west.\n\nAs the high pressure shifts southeast of the region this afternoon, \nsoutherly flow will support warmer temperatures, but a few decaying\nshowers and cloud cover will act to slow the warmup. Have trended \ntemperatures a little cooler with highs from the upper 60s north to \nthe lower 70s south. \n\nA period of warm and muggy conditions develops Saturday into Sunday.\nTemperature forecasts Saturday vary across the local area and will \nbe dependent on lingering cloud cover leftover from Friday night and \nSaturday morning showers. Have trended temperatures slightly cooler \nwith highs from the upper 70s to the lower 80s. \n\nA warm front lifts northward Sunday as a mid-level ridge builds over\nthe eastern CONUS, with temperatures rising into the mid to upper \n80s and perhaps a few lower 90s. Similar conditions are forecast \nMonday ahead of an approaching cold front, but mostly sunny skies \nwill allow for a few more observations in the lower 90s - especially\nover the Scioto River Valley. Based on thermal profiles and 8H\ntemperatures have trended NBM highs cooler to reach these solutions.  \n\nThe timing of the next cold front Tuesday night or Wednesday \ncontinues to offer some uncertainty regarding the temperature \nforecast for Tuesday. A faster front would reduce the temperatures on\nTuesday, and a slower front supports the potential for another day \nin the mid to upper 80s and lower 90s. \n\nThe passage of the cold front Tuesday night or Wednesday results in\ncooler conditions Wednesday, although temperatures are still around\nseasonal normal values. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2) \n\nDry conditions remain in place over the area this morning, however, \na compact shortwave moving through the lower Midwest has led to the \ndevelopment of thunderstorms west of the area. These storms will be \nin a decaying state but will offer a low chance for a shower across \neastern Indiana, southwest Ohio, and northern Kentucky into the early\nafternoon. Forecast soundings indicate that the air mass ahead of \nthis feature will be quite dry so some of this will likely end up as \nsprinkles.\n\nMoisture increases over the Ohio Valley tonight, supporting the \npotential for elevated showers into the west late. Within the warm \nascent region, another weak shortwave will eject eastward into the \nregion. Forecast soundings indicate elevated instability so some \nrumbles of thunder are possible into Saturday morning. \n\nAnother shortwave moves through the region Saturday afternoon into \nthe evening. Lingering cloud cover left over from Saturday morning\nmay limit the severe threat. Most of the CAM solutions generally \nshow the development of deep convection. Given effective shear of\n25-30 kts and moderate instability the potential exists for a strong\nto severe thunderstorm threat. Scattered thunderstorms capable of \ndamaging wind gusts or large hail will be the main threats.\n\nThe threat for scattered showers and thunderstorms continues into\nearly Sunday before the front lifts northward.",
